{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"gap-02-db-ivm-type-markers-and-empty-window-moves","children":[{"type":"text","value":"GAP-02: db-ivm type markers and empty-window moves"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed executable revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a921e760"},{"type":"text","value":" (parent "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"33a19494"},{"type":"text","value":", the fetched\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin/main"},{"type":"text","value":" at review time). This record and its changeset follow in a\ndocumentation-only commit. The reviewed revision changes tests and the coverage\nmap. It does not change production code."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"contract-path-and-limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract, path, and limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The structural hash owns sampled distinctions between container kinds. For\neach generated integer sequence, the test compares a Set or Map with a plain\nobject that has the same indexed entries. The independent expectation is that\nthe two container kinds produce different fingerprints in this sampled domain.\nThe test invokes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hash"},{"type":"text","value":" through the native hash session and compares each pair\nafter hashing. A 32-bit hash can collide, so this is not an injectivity claim."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The fractional-index top-K operator reports a changed window through an empty\nD2 output message when no relation rows move. The fixed history inserts one\nrow, retracts it, moves the requested window, and runs the graph. The test\nchecks the exact output-message suffix at that checkpoint. It does not run a\nlazy source or establish that downstream acquisition starts."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"grammar-controls-and-calibration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grammar controls and calibration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hash grammar generates one to five unique integers, including negative,\nzero, and positive values. The same values construct both compared carriers.\nThe one-value "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[[0]]"},{"type":"text","value":" counterexample reconstructs the reported type-marker\nfault for Set and Map. Unique values are necessary: duplicate Set values or\nMap keys would make the indexed-object comparison cease to represent matching\nentries. The carrier axis distinguishes Set from Map. Removing either carrier\nwould leave that type marker unchecked. Length one is the marginal case; the\ngenerator also covers longer sequences. Empty carriers, duplicate entries, and\narbitrary object keys are outside this law. The fixed top-K history has no\ngenerated grammar."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On unchanged production, the focused tests passed. A temporary hash mutant\nreplaced "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasher.update(marker)"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hasher.update(0)"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hashPlainObject"},{"type":"text","value":".\nBoth Set/object and Map/object properties failed in fixed and random campaigns.\nThe outcome class was "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure"}]},{"type":"text","value":", not setup failure or timeout. The\nfixed campaign reported seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1659001"},{"type":"text","value":", path "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0:0:0"},{"type":"text","value":", and minimized values\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[0]"},{"type":"text","value":" for both carriers. Direct replay with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_IVM_HASH_TYPE_SEED=1659001"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_IVM_HASH_TYPE_PATH=0:0:0"},{"type":"text","value":" failed at the same comparison. Hash\ninitialization changes numeric fingerprints between processes, but the\ncounterexample and equality failure remain the same."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A temporary top-K mutant suppressed output when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TopKState.isEmpty"},{"type":"text","value":" was true.\nThe fixed test reached its final assertion and received "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"},{"type":"text","value":" messages instead\nof the required "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[[]]"},{"type":"text","value":". Its outcome class was "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Both\nproduction files were restored before the full green run."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-001-through-orc-012","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 through ORC-012"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hash owner's opening contract states fingerprint scope and collision limits. The top-K test names the empty-window report and its lazy-source motivation. The coverage map records both limits."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hash expectation comes from equivalent indexed entries with different carrier kinds. The top-K expectation comes from the window-change report, without importing its change classifier."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hash file shows the contract, generated values, relation, native driver, and comparison. The top-K addition is a focused regression beside the existing top-K relation owner."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hash grammar reconstruction, carrier ablation, value range, and duplicate-entry exclusion appear above. The top-K regression is fixed, so the generated-grammar trigger does not apply."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hash"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"topKWithFractionalIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" execute on production paths. The tests compare the hashes and exact D2 message suffix at their stated checkpoints."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both named wrong designs failed by assertion at the intended checkpoints, as recorded above."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each hash carrier runs the same property for 100 cases with fixed seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1659001"},{"type":"text","value":" and a random seed. The seed and shrink path select a direct replay. The property does not use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fc.commands"},{"type":"text","value":". The top-K regression is not generated."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No stateful reference model changes."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No model-only terms combine or split glossary concepts."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The existing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HashReplayError"},{"type":"text","value":" retains the law, input, observations, initialization tape, and original cause. The native fast-check report retains seed and path. Neither test acquires an external resource."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No named shared semantic fault calls for a second formulation. The two carrier comparisons and hostile mutants calibrate the stated laws."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record ties the reviewed executable revision, the outcome classes, grammar controls, and limits to the coverage map."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"verification","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verification"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"From "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db-ivm"},{"type":"text","value":", the package Vitest binary ran all 39 test files:\n564 tests passed with no type errors. The package TypeScript check passed.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" passed. The package uses the normal full test campaign;\nthe four new hash campaigns run as part of that command. The test environment\nused Node "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"v24.19.0"},{"type":"text","value":", Vitest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"3.2.4"},{"type":"text","value":", and fast-check "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"3.23.2"},{"type":"text","value":" on Darwin arm64."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"replay-harness-follow-up","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replay-harness follow-up"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed executable revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8653d2a9"},{"type":"text","value":". This follow-up changes only the hash\noracle's replay configuration and avoids building Map entries during Set runs.\nThe contract, generator, native hash driver, and comparison above stay the same."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before the follow-up, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_IVM_HASH_TYPE_SEED=garbage"},{"type":"text","value":" ran a selected\ncarrier test successfully. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Number('garbage')"},{"type":"text","value":" produced "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":", which fast-check\ncoerced to seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":". A large integer such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"4294967297"},{"type":"text","value":" also changed seed\nthrough fast-check's 32-bit conversion. A path without a seed failed during\nmodule collection, so Vitest ran no tests from the file. After the follow-up,\nthese invalid settings fail only the Set and Map replay tests with a direct\nconfiguration error. A replay seed must be a 32-bit integer. The other 40 hash\ntests still run and pass. A valid seed and path pass both replay tests, and the\nordinary file run passes all 44 tests. This strengthens the direct-replay\ninterface required by ORC-007 without changing the oracle law."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The full db-ivm run at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"8653d2a9"},{"type":"text","value":" passed all 564 tests in 39 files with no type\nerrors. The package TypeScript check and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" passed. The earlier\nwrong-result controls remain the ORC-006 calibration for the unchanged hash\ncomparison and empty-window assertion."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"gap-02-db-ivm-type-markers-and-empty-window-moves","text":"GAP-02: db-ivm type markers and empty-window moves","level":1},{"id":"contract-path-and-limits","text":"Contract, path, and limits","level":2},{"id":"grammar-controls-and-calibration","text":"Grammar controls and calibration","level":2},{"id":"orc-001-through-orc-012","text":"ORC-001 through ORC-012","level":2},{"id":"verification","text":"Verification","level":2},{"id":"replay-harness-follow-up","text":"Replay-harness follow-up","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# GAP-02: db-ivm type markers and empty-window moves\n\nReviewed executable revision: `a921e760` (parent `33a19494`, the fetched\n`origin/main` at review time). This record and its changeset follow in a\ndocumentation-only commit. The reviewed revision changes tests and the coverage\nmap. It does not change production code.\n\n## Contract, path, and limits\n\nThe structural hash owns sampled distinctions between container kinds. For\neach generated integer sequence, the test compares a Set or Map with a plain\nobject that has the same indexed entries. The independent expectation is that\nthe two container kinds produce different fingerprints in this sampled domain.\nThe test invokes `hash` through the native hash session and compares each pair\nafter hashing. A 32-bit hash can collide, so this is not an injectivity claim.\n\nThe fractional-index top-K operator reports a changed window through an empty\nD2 output message when no relation rows move. The fixed history inserts one\nrow, retracts it, moves the requested window, and runs the graph. The test\nchecks the exact output-message suffix at that checkpoint. It does not run a\nlazy source or establish that downstream acquisition starts.\n\n## Grammar controls and calibration\n\nThe hash grammar generates one to five unique integers, including negative,\nzero, and positive values. The same values construct both compared carriers.\nThe one-value `[[0]]` counterexample reconstructs the reported type-marker\nfault for Set and Map. Unique values are necessary: duplicate Set values or\nMap keys would make the indexed-object comparison cease to represent matching\nentries. The carrier axis distinguishes Set from Map. Removing either carrier\nwould leave that type marker unchecked. Length one is the marginal case; the\ngenerator also covers longer sequences. Empty carriers, duplicate entries, and\narbitrary object keys are outside this law. The fixed top-K history has no\ngenerated grammar.\n\nOn unchanged production, the focused tests passed. A temporary hash mutant\nreplaced `hasher.update(marker)` with `hasher.update(0)` in `hashPlainObject`.\nBoth Set/object and Map/object properties failed in fixed and random campaigns.\nThe outcome class was **assertion failure**, not setup failure or timeout. The\nfixed campaign reported seed `1659001`, path `0:0:0`, and minimized values\n`[0]` for both carriers. Direct replay with\n`TANSTACK_DB_IVM_HASH_TYPE_SEED=1659001` and\n`TANSTACK_DB_IVM_HASH_TYPE_PATH=0:0:0` failed at the same comparison. Hash\ninitialization changes numeric fingerprints between processes, but the\ncounterexample and equality failure remain the same.\n\nA temporary top-K mutant suppressed output when `TopKState.isEmpty` was true.\nThe fixed test reached its final assertion and received `[]` messages instead\nof the required `[[]]`. Its outcome class was **assertion failure**. Both\nproduction files were restored before the full green run.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-012\n\n| Requirement | Result                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| ORC-001     | The hash owner's opening contract states fingerprint scope and collision limits. The top-K test names the empty-window report and its lazy-source motivation. The coverage map records both limits.                                  |\n| ORC-002     | The hash expectation comes from equivalent indexed entries with different carrier kinds. The top-K expectation comes from the window-change report, without importing its change classifier.                                         |\n| ORC-003     | The hash file shows the contract, generated values, relation, native driver, and comparison. The top-K addition is a focused regression beside the existing top-K relation owner.                                                    |\n| ORC-004     | The hash grammar reconstruction, carrier ablation, value range, and duplicate-entry exclusion appear above. The top-K regression is fixed, so the generated-grammar trigger does not apply.                                          |\n| ORC-005     | `hash` and `topKWithFractionalIndex` execute on production paths. The tests compare the hashes and exact D2 message suffix at their stated checkpoints.                                                                              |\n| ORC-006     | Both named wrong designs failed by assertion at the intended checkpoints, as recorded above.                                                                                                                                         |\n| ORC-007     | Each hash carrier runs the same property for 100 cases with fixed seed `1659001` and a random seed. The seed and shrink path select a direct replay. The property does not use `fc.commands`. The top-K regression is not generated. |\n| ORC-008     | No stateful reference model changes.                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| ORC-009     | No model-only terms combine or split glossary concepts.                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ORC-010     | The existing `HashReplayError` retains the law, input, observations, initialization tape, and original cause. The native fast-check report retains seed and path. Neither test acquires an external resource.                        |\n| ORC-011     | No named shared semantic fault calls for a second formulation. The two carrier comparisons and hostile mutants calibrate the stated laws.                                                                                            |\n| ORC-012     | This record ties the reviewed executable revision, the outcome classes, grammar controls, and limits to the coverage map.                                                                                                            |\n\n## Verification\n\nFrom `packages/db-ivm`, the package Vitest binary ran all 39 test files:\n564 tests passed with no type errors. The package TypeScript check passed.\n`git diff --check` passed. The package uses the normal full test campaign;\nthe four new hash campaigns run as part of that command. The test environment\nused Node `v24.19.0`, Vitest `3.2.4`, and fast-check `3.23.2` on Darwin arm64.\n\n## Replay-harness follow-up\n\nReviewed executable revision: `8653d2a9`. This follow-up changes only the hash\noracle's replay configuration and avoids building Map entries during Set runs.\nThe contract, generator, native hash driver, and comparison above stay the same.\n\nBefore the follow-up, `TANSTACK_DB_IVM_HASH_TYPE_SEED=garbage` ran a selected\ncarrier test successfully. `Number('garbage')` produced `NaN`, which fast-check\ncoerced to seed `0`. A large integer such as `4294967297` also changed seed\nthrough fast-check's 32-bit conversion. A path without a seed failed during\nmodule collection, so Vitest ran no tests from the file. After the follow-up,\nthese invalid settings fail only the Set and Map replay tests with a direct\nconfiguration error. A replay seed must be a 32-bit integer. The other 40 hash\ntests still run and pass. A valid seed and path pass both replay tests, and the\nordinary file run passes all 44 tests. This strengthens the direct-replay\ninterface required by ORC-007 without changing the oracle law.\n\nThe full db-ivm run at `8653d2a9` passed all 564 tests in 39 files with no type\nerrors. The package TypeScript check and `git diff --check` passed. The earlier\nwrong-result controls remain the ORC-006 calibration for the unchanged hash\ncomparison and empty-window assertion.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# GAP-02: db-ivm type markers and empty-window moves\n\nReviewed executable revision: `a921e760` (parent `33a19494`, the fetched\n`origin/main` at review time). This record and its changeset follow in a\ndocumentation-only commit. The reviewed revision changes tests and the coverage\nmap. It does not change production code.\n\n## Contract, path, and limits\n\nThe structural hash owns sampled distinctions between container kinds. For\neach generated integer sequence, the test compares a Set or Map with a plain\nobject that has the same indexed entries. The independent expectation is that\nthe two container kinds produce different fingerprints in this sampled domain.\nThe test invokes `hash` through the native hash session and compares each pair\nafter hashing. A 32-bit hash can collide, so this is not an injectivity claim.\n\nThe fractional-index top-K operator reports a changed window through an empty\nD2 output message when no relation rows move. The fixed history inserts one\nrow, retracts it, moves the requested window, and runs the graph. The test\nchecks the exact output-message suffix at that checkpoint. It does not run a\nlazy source or establish that downstream acquisition starts.\n\n## Grammar controls and calibration\n\nThe hash grammar generates one to five unique integers, including negative,\nzero, and positive values. The same values construct both compared carriers.\nThe one-value `[[0]]` counterexample reconstructs the reported type-marker\nfault for Set and Map. Unique values are necessary: duplicate Set values or\nMap keys would make the indexed-object comparison cease to represent matching\nentries. The carrier axis distinguishes Set from Map. Removing either carrier\nwould leave that type marker unchecked. Length one is the marginal case; the\ngenerator also covers longer sequences. Empty carriers, duplicate entries, and\narbitrary object keys are outside this law. The fixed top-K history has no\ngenerated grammar.\n\nOn unchanged production, the focused tests passed. A temporary hash mutant\nreplaced `hasher.update(marker)` with `hasher.update(0)` in `hashPlainObject`.\nBoth Set/object and Map/object properties failed in fixed and random campaigns.\nThe outcome class was **assertion failure**, not setup failure or timeout. The\nfixed campaign reported seed `1659001`, path `0:0:0`, and minimized values\n`[0]` for both carriers. Direct replay with\n`TANSTACK_DB_IVM_HASH_TYPE_SEED=1659001` and\n`TANSTACK_DB_IVM_HASH_TYPE_PATH=0:0:0` failed at the same comparison. Hash\ninitialization changes numeric fingerprints between processes, but the\ncounterexample and equality failure remain the same.\n\nA temporary top-K mutant suppressed output when `TopKState.isEmpty` was true.\nThe fixed test reached its final assertion and received `[]` messages instead\nof the required `[[]]`. Its outcome class was **assertion failure**. Both\nproduction files were restored before the full green run.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-012\n\n| Requirement | Result                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| ORC-001     | The hash owner's opening contract states fingerprint scope and collision limits. The top-K test names the empty-window report and its lazy-source motivation. The coverage map records both limits.                                  |\n| ORC-002     | The hash expectation comes from equivalent indexed entries with different carrier kinds. The top-K expectation comes from the window-change report, without importing its change classifier.                                         |\n| ORC-003     | The hash file shows the contract, generated values, relation, native driver, and comparison. The top-K addition is a focused regression beside the existing top-K relation owner.                                                    |\n| ORC-004     | The hash grammar reconstruction, carrier ablation, value range, and duplicate-entry exclusion appear above. The top-K regression is fixed, so the generated-grammar trigger does not apply.                                          |\n| ORC-005     | `hash` and `topKWithFractionalIndex` execute on production paths. The tests compare the hashes and exact D2 message suffix at their stated checkpoints.                                                                              |\n| ORC-006     | Both named wrong designs failed by assertion at the intended checkpoints, as recorded above.                                                                                                                                         |\n| ORC-007     | Each hash carrier runs the same property for 100 cases with fixed seed `1659001` and a random seed. The seed and shrink path select a direct replay. The property does not use `fc.commands`. The top-K regression is not generated. |\n| ORC-008     | No stateful reference model changes.                                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| ORC-009     | No model-only terms combine or split glossary concepts.                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ORC-010     | The existing `HashReplayError` retains the law, input, observations, initialization tape, and original cause. The native fast-check report retains seed and path. Neither test acquires an external resource.                        |\n| ORC-011     | No named shared semantic fault calls for a second formulation. The two carrier comparisons and hostile mutants calibrate the stated laws.                                                                                            |\n| ORC-012     | This record ties the reviewed executable revision, the outcome classes, grammar controls, and limits to the coverage map.                                                                                                            |\n\n## Verification\n\nFrom `packages/db-ivm`, the package Vitest binary ran all 39 test files:\n564 tests passed with no type errors. The package TypeScript check passed.\n`git diff --check` passed. The package uses the normal full test campaign;\nthe four new hash campaigns run as part of that command. The test environment\nused Node `v24.19.0`, Vitest `3.2.4`, and fast-check `3.23.2` on Darwin arm64.\n\n## Replay-harness follow-up\n\nReviewed executable revision: `8653d2a9`. This follow-up changes only the hash\noracle's replay configuration and avoids building Map entries during Set runs.\nThe contract, generator, native hash driver, and comparison above stay the same.\n\nBefore the follow-up, `TANSTACK_DB_IVM_HASH_TYPE_SEED=garbage` ran a selected\ncarrier test successfully. `Number('garbage')` produced `NaN`, which fast-check\ncoerced to seed `0`. A large integer such as `4294967297` also changed seed\nthrough fast-check's 32-bit conversion. A path without a seed failed during\nmodule collection, so Vitest ran no tests from the file. After the follow-up,\nthese invalid settings fail only the Set and Map replay tests with a direct\nconfiguration error. A replay seed must be a 32-bit integer. The other 40 hash\ntests still run and pass. A valid seed and path pass both replay tests, and the\nordinary file run passes all 44 tests. This strengthens the direct-replay\ninterface required by ORC-007 without changing the oracle law.\n\nThe full db-ivm run at `8653d2a9` passed all 564 tests in 39 files with no type\nerrors. The package TypeScript check and `git diff --check` passed. The earlier\nwrong-result controls remain the ORC-006 calibration for the unchanged hash\ncomparison and empty-window assertion.\n"}
